.TH RESET_PCIE_AL9.SH 1 "2026-10-06" "otsdaq-mu2e 11.03.00" "Mu2e DAQ Manual"
.SH NAME
reset_PCIe_AL9.sh \- unload the mu2e driver, remove and rescan the DTC/CFO
PCIe devices, reload the driver and print their firmware versions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo reset_PCIe_AL9.sh
.RB [ NOKILL ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B reset_PCIe_AL9.sh
brings a reprogrammed DTC, CFO or RTF board back onto the PCIe bus.
It must run as root, but it does not check that it does.
There is no confirmation prompt and no dry-run mode.
The steps are:
.IP 1. 4
.B Sweep.
It sends
.B kill \-9
to every process whose
.B ps aux
line contains
.BR reset_PCIe .
Two kinds of line are spared: a line that contains this process's PID
anywhere as a substring, and a line that contains the word
.BR sudo .
See
.BR BUGS .
.IP 2. 4
.B Lock.
It creates
.I /tmp/mu2e.lock
as a symbolic link to its PID.
If a live process holds the lock, the script waits 2 s between checks, up to
31 times, and then exits 1.
There is one exception: if the holder is the only running
.IR program_*AL9.sh
or
.IR boot_from_flash*AL9.sh
other than
.IR program_all_FPGA_AL9.sh ,
the script treats the holder as its caller and proceeds while the caller
holds the lock.
A stale lock is removed once.
The lock is removed on exit.
.IP 3. 4
.B Unload.
This step runs only if
.B lspci
lists a device matching
.B Xilinx.*704[23]
(device ID 7042 or 7043).
If none matches, it prints "No ... cards found" and goes to step 4.
.RS 4
.IP a. 3
Unless
.B NOKILL
is given, it sends
.B kill \-9
to every process holding
.I /dev/mu2e*
(per
.BR lsof ),
up to 8 rounds 3 s apart.
It then runs
.B killall \-9 xdaq.exe
and
.BR "killall \-9 boardreader" .
These kill every such process on the node, for every user and every
partition, whether or not it uses the card.
.IP b. 3
It waits 3 s and runs
.BR "rmmod mu2e" .
If
.B lsmod
still lists
.BR mu2e ,
it repeats a and b with no limit.
See
.BR BUGS .
.IP c. 3
It writes
.B 1
to
.IR /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000: BDF /remove
for each matching device.
.RE
.IP 4. 4
.B Rescan.
It writes
.B 1
to
.I /sys/bus/pci/rescan
and runs
.BR "modprobe mu2e" .
.IP 5. 4
.B Report.
It changes to
.IR /root ,
sources
.I ./setup_pcie_AL9.sh
(which is not in this repository), and prints
.B my_cntl \-d 0 read 0x9004
and
.B my_cntl \-d 1 read 0x9004
(the firmware version register of devices 0 and 1).
.PP
.BR program_all_FPGA_AL9.sh (1),
.BR program_flash_all_FPGA_AL9.sh (1)
and
.BR boot_from_flash_all_DTCs_AL9.sh (1)
call this script through
.B sudo
unless they are given
.BR NORESET .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B NOKILL
Skips step 3a only: the device-holder kills and the two
.B killall
calls.
It does not skip the sweep in step 1, the module unload, the device removal
or the rescan.
.B rmmod mu2e
fails while any process holds
.IR /dev/mu2e* .
With
.BR NOKILL ,
the reset therefore completes only when nothing has the device open, and is
then the same as a reset without the flag.
If anything does hold the device, the script loops forever.
Check first with
.BR "lsof /dev/mu2e*" .
.PP
The shifter procedure says of this flag: "It skips killing running processes
before resetting the PCIe cards".
That is accurate.
The procedure's advice to pass it "when others may be using the system"
describes exactly the case in which the reset cannot finish.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /tmp/mu2e.lock
Symbolic link to the holder's PID.
It is shared with
.IR program_one_FPGA_AL9.sh ,
.IR program_both_DTCs_AL9.sh ,
.I boot_from_flash_both_DTCs_AL9.sh
and mu2edaq-operations
.IR read_dtc_temps.sh .
.TP
.I /dev/mu2e*
Character devices of the
.B mu2e
driver (mu2e-pcie-utils).
.TP
.IR /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000: BDF /remove ", " /sys/bus/pci/rescan
Written in steps 3c and 4.
.TP
.I /root/setup_pcie_AL9.sh
Puts
.B my_cntl
on the
.BR PATH .
It is node-local and not version-controlled here.
.TP
.I reset_PCIe_AL9_sudowrapper.sh
A three-line wrapper in this directory.
Its comment says to install it as
.IR ~mu2ehwdev/reset_PCIe_AL9.sh .
It runs:
.EX
sudo /home/mu2ehwdev/ots_spack/srcs/otsdaq\-mu2e/tools/reset_PCIe_AL9.sh "$@"
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 1
The lock could not be acquired.
.TP
.B 0
Every other case, including no matching card, a failed
.BR modprobe ,
and
.B my_cntl
errors.
The "mu2e kernel module failed to unload" exit is unreachable in practice,
because the loop in step 3b ends only when the module is gone.
.SH EXAMPLES
CFO, Tracker, CRV and STM nodes, from the shifter procedure:
.PP
.EX
sudo /home/mu2ehwdev/reset_PCIe_AL9.sh NOKILL
.EE
.PP
Before relying on
.BR NOKILL ,
see whether anything holds the device:
.PP
.EX
lsof /dev/mu2e*
.EE
.PP
Calorimeter nodes, as root over ssh, in parallel:
.PP
.EX
for node in calo\-{01..12} calo\-14; do
    ssh root@mu2e\-$node bash /home/mu2ehwdev/reset_PCIe_AL9.sh &
done; wait
.EE
.SH BUGS
The unload loop never ends if the module cannot be removed.
.B TRIES=3
is tested with
.BR "expr $TRIES \- 1" ,
but nothing decrements it.
Three cases trigger this:
.B NOKILL
while a process holds the device; running without root; and a holder that
survives
.BR "kill \-9" ,
for example one in uninterruptible sleep.
The loop prints every 3 s and keeps
.I /tmp/mu2e.lock
held.
Interrupt it with Ctrl-C.
.PP
The sweep in step 1 matches its own sudo wrapper.
Suppose
.I ~mu2ehwdev/reset_PCIe_AL9.sh
is the wrapper, as the wrapper's comment instructs.
Then the wrapper's
.B bash
process is killed by the real script.
The outer
.B sudo
returns status 137, and the prompt comes back while the reset continues in
the background.
A second invocation started at that point kills the first.
The PID exclusion is also a substring match, so an unrelated PID that
contains this PID's digits is spared.
